WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.223
Human-in-the-loop methods improve VLA policies through expert corrections,

00:00:07.345 --> 00:00:11.470
but collecting those corrections and repeatedly evaluating on real robots

00:00:11.471 --> 00:00:14.790
require substantial human effort and hardware use.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:19.592
To lower the cost of exploring and developing HIL algorithms,

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.915
we introduce SimPT, a simulation framework that automates policy rollouts,

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:29.888
corrective data collection, policy post-training, and standalone evaluation.

00:00:30.841 --> 00:00:33.810
At its core is automated recovery after failure.

00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:37.287
Automating recovery requires deciding

00:00:37.288 --> 00:00:39.209
where to resume a failed rollout,

00:00:39.318 --> 00:00:42.408
and how the expert should complete the task from that state.

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:45.611
Since both depend on task progress,

00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:49.079
SimPT uses an Agent-Compiled Progress Graph.

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:54.512
GPT agents first compile candidate graphs from task information,

00:00:54.513 --> 00:00:59.137
and the selected graph is validated and calibrated before use.

00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:02.795
During execution, fixed checks on privileged simulator state

00:01:02.796 --> 00:01:08.236
track progress and retain stable snapshots, without further agent queries.

00:01:08.266 --> 00:01:12.500
After a rollout fails, SimPT invokes the programmatic expert

00:01:12.501 --> 00:01:17.134
from advanced snapshots and falls back to earlier stages as needed.

00:01:17.164 --> 00:01:21.209
Successful and failed rollouts, plus corrective trajectories,

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.706
form the post-training buffer.

00:01:23.736 --> 00:01:25.739
This creates a repeatable pipeline

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.887
whose standalone evaluation compares policies under controlled conditions.

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:36.205
This repeatability supports comparisons at two levels.

00:01:36.235 --> 00:01:40.577
For recovery, the Progress Graph requires seventy-five percent fewer attempts

00:01:40.578 --> 00:01:43.517
than the fixed-interval Naive baseline.

00:01:43.547 --> 00:01:47.095
For learning, SimPT enables us to implement and compare

00:01:47.096 --> 00:01:50.695
four post-training methods across seven tasks.

00:01:50.725 --> 00:01:54.878
Using SimPT, we develop RECAP-CF-LPF,

00:01:54.879 --> 00:01:57.485
a refinement of RECAP that combines cross-fitting

00:01:57.486 --> 00:02:00.869
and low-pass filtering of value predictions.

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:04.488
This refinement improves overall held-out simulation success

00:02:04.489 --> 00:02:07.872
by two point eight six percentage points.

00:02:07.902 --> 00:02:12.364
We then apply it to real-robot human-in-the-loop post-training.

00:02:13.183 --> 00:02:16.704
Across twenty-five Ethernet cable-insertion scenarios,

00:02:16.705 --> 00:02:22.592
RECAP-CF-LPF increases success from seventy-two to ninety-six percent.

00:02:22.622 --> 00:02:24.785
Together, these results close the loop

00:02:24.786 --> 00:02:28.737
from systematic simulation experiments to real-robot validation,

00:02:28.738 --> 00:02:32.379
demonstrating SimPT's utility for exploring and developing

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:36.546
intervention-based VLA post-training algorithms.
